  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unsettled today and tomorrow, mainly for the \nnorthern half of the region, but severe weather and flash \nflooding is not expected. \n\nA surface low is currently off the New Jersey coast and lifting\ntoward Long Island. The surface low will slowly move northward \nas the day goes on, with our region on the backside of the low. \nAn upper level low will be positioned over New England, with our\narea being under cyclonic flow aloft. As a shortwave rotates \nthrough and with some wrap-around moisture in place, some \nshowers and thunderstorms could develop this afternoon, mainly \nnorth of I-78. Not much instability or shear to work with, and \nguidance has really cut back in coverage of \nshowers/thunderstorms compared to a day ago, so not much is \nexpected in terms of impacts. In fact, the Flood Watch\noriginally posted through most of the day was cancelled early. \n\nA few showers could linger into the night, with another round \npotentially developing on Thursday, again mainly for the Poconos\nand North Jersey. This should be the final round before the \nupper level low and associated surface low move out.\n\nAreas from Philadelphia on south should actually stay dry but\ncannot rule out an isolated shower over SE PA or South Jersey\ntoday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Brief period of quiet weather to end the week \nand for the first half of the weekend before another system \ncomes in on Sunday. \n\nHigh pressure begins to take over on Thursday Night and will\nremain in place through Saturday Night. Seasonable temperatures\nare expected and it should be an overall nice stretch of days. \n\nA frontal system will move in on Sunday and Monday, as we\ntransition back to an unsettled pattern to start August. Still\ntoo early to discern what specific impacts will be but this\nperiod bears some watching. Details will be ironed out in\nsubsequent forecast cycles.",
